## 1. Scope

This reference covers composite tubes built from a vulcanized fibre element and a fibre reinforced structural wall. The composite exists because no single material does both jobs well: the liner has to generate gas under the arc, and the outer wall has to carry the pressure.

## 2. Construction

- Inner vulcanized fibre liner, which releases gas when exposed to the arc and helps extinguish it.
- Outer structural wall in resin impregnated glass fibre carrying the mechanical load during interruption.
- The interface between liner and outer wall is the critical bond line.
- Tubes are machined at the ends after the composite is built.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Configuration | Vulcanized fibre liner with a fibre reinforced structural wall | Liner and wall are specified separately and built to the fuse design. |
| Vulcanized fibre | Material per ASTM D710 | Moisture content of the fibre affects both the arc behaviour and the dimensions. |
| Burst strength | Established by type test on the complete fuse | The composite is only proven as part of the fuse assembly. |
| Environmental limits | Humidity dependent for the fibre element | Moisture absorption is the reason composite designs are often selected over plain fibre. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- Whether the fuse design calls for a composite at all, or whether a single material meets the duty.
- The gas generating requirement inside the bore, which sets the liner material and thickness.
- The pressure duty, which sets the structural wall.
- Climatic conditions at the installation, since fibre moisture behaviour is the reason composites were developed.
- Whether the tube is a replacement for an existing design and has to match an existing drawing.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- ASTM D710, vulcanized fibre sheets and related forms.
- IEC 60282-2, high voltage fuses for expulsion fuse designs.
- IEEE C37.41, design tests for high voltage fuses.
- IEC 60893 series, rigid laminated sheets based on thermosetting resins.
- IEC 60587, tracking and erosion test for electrical insulating materials used under severe conditions.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Keep the fibre element sealed until the fuse is assembled. Moisture picked up in storage changes dimensions and arc behaviour.
- Protect the liner bore from mechanical damage during assembly of the fuse.
- Do not allow the tube to sit in a damp area during the assembly process.
- Confirm bond quality on a sample from each batch where the liner to wall interface is a production step.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Liner to wall debonding | Contamination at the interface or moisture in the fibre before bonding | Control humidity in the assembly area and verify bond quality on a batch sample. |
| Arc erosion of the liner | Liner material or thickness not matched to the fault energy | Set the liner against the fuse design and confirm through type test. |
| Dimensional change in storage | Fibre element taking up moisture | Seal the tubes for storage and assemble from dry stock. |
| Burst during interruption | Structural wall thinner than the duty requires | Confirm the wall against the type tested design. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Expulsion and current limiting fuse designs where a composite body is specified.
- Dropout cutout tubes in distribution networks.
- Breaker and interrupter components requiring a gas generating element.
- Electrical insulation converters building fuse assemblies.

